 1950s and saw this  robust well welfare state social welfare  state um that she said that sri lanka  was  eating the fruit before growing the tree  that it was spending rather than  saving for the short term and so i think  one of the issues that we see people  like mark hassen deal with in academic  and formal language and we see others  like mobile hack deal with in a kind of  more political vein is this other  dimension of inequality which is how do  you balance the the current against the  future  and it's plausible to think that with  our current interest in  um  in global inequalities certainly since  2008 um  even just an economic term that that  become central i would say that the this  group  for all of their insights the ones who  i'm looking at we're not necessarily the  right ones to look at the um  the interface of structures of economic  inequality with other structures of  inequality uh and  it's  two kind of vexing questions to answer  directly  um right now but i would say that when  you ask economists to talk about  inequality it's not a surprise that they  turn to economic inequality and so  perhaps the message is one of amnas  about bringing in um people working on  boundary you know  on the boundaries or economics and its  neighbors  as a way  to kind of continue this process moving  forward  thanks david amna can i bring you in  next  sure actually david that's a great segue  for me um so one of the things i was  going to say is that what the sort of  what was i mean um the commoner model  obviously has could be different  problems but the one thing um that it  was doing was that it was  interdisciplinary in nature right but it  wasn't just economists who were um  producing knowledge but it was also  anthropologists sociologists and  political scientists um who were there  and really like  they were living in formula they were  going out into the communities and  working with people and producing these  forms of knowledge so i think maybe um  