hat engagement  i think is not only not only not only  leads to a good policy making and so but  it also actually leads to good economics  and defined in its own in its own terms  so let me leave it at that  agreed and and that's part of why i'm so  grateful that rohini and jerry have  brought us together because it's  addressing some of those problems you  just articulated um this this discipline  not engaging with its own history enough  um and not engaging enough with other  disciplines and i think this this  opening session of the symposium and the  whole symposium will be really valuable  from that perspective so uh rohini jerry  you kind of kicked us off in terms of  why this conversation why now and would  love just any final reflections from the  two of you and maybe connecting this  conversation that we're having today to  the larger symposium jerry you want to  start  okay well just just very briefly i think  that  the last comments that were made um  kind of would take us back to i think  david was uh talking about someone among  his six who um  mentioned that at cambridge the  economist back in the um i guess the 40s  or 50s weren't asking the right q  questions well  at least they weren't asking all the  right questions and  that's where i think the rest of the  symposium is going to be trying to go  we've tried to bring together people who  are asking questions that uh  that are somewhat different from what  we're usually going to see um  in economics journals and part of the  reason for that i i would say  which connects to david's point and the  one that robbie just made as well as  amna is that  one gets rewards  in the economics profession  by  publishing in certain kinds of journals  and you publish in certain kinds of  generals by  on the basis of  methodological sophistication  so when you start asking why are  economists interfacing with economic  history  and with anthropologists or political  scientists more  that kind is already your answer 